  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nA little warmer start this morning with morning temperatures in the \n30s and 40s due to mid and upper level clouds rolling in from the \nnorthwest. A weak cold front will be moving in from the northwest \nlater today with the pressure gradient relaxing behind the\nboundary. Not expecting any rain with the frontal passage as \nthe vertical profiles remain mostly dry through the atmosphere.\nDespite the cold front advancing in from the the northwest this\nafternoon we should still see some fairly nice high temperatures\ndue to the region getting a warm start in the morning. Highs \nare expected to be in the 60s. \n\nThrough the work week:\n\nHigh pressure becomes the dominant feature through much of the \nupcoming work week. Unseasonably mild and dry weather will take hold \nacross the area as an upper level ridge anchors over the central \npart of CONUS, setting up an omega blocking pattern. High temps will \nfluctuate between 50s and 60s through the week with overnight lows \nin the 30s and 40s. Lake breezes at times will keep shoreline \nlocations closer to seasonal norms for mid October.\n\nLate work week into the weekend:\n\nThe question of when our next best chance for precipitation will be \nis highly uncertain. Global models and ensembles suggest the \npotential for this omega block to break down on Friday into next \nweekend, with a cutoff low over southern California and the Great \nBasin propagating northeast towards the Midwest as a shortwave moves \nthrough the Pacific Northwest and Northern Rockies. However, \nconfidence in this timing remains very low given how far out in the \nforecast period this is and the tendency for omega blocks to be slow \nto break down. Furthered evidenced by the NBM PoPs continuing to be \ndelayed with every update over the past four forecast cycles.",
